<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="unit3a-data-representation-and-compression">
  <time minutes="90" />
  <title>Digital Information and Data Compression (2026)</title>
  <introduction>
    <p>
      Solving problems with computers often involves working with large amounts of data. In this lesson we explore how to reduce the size of data to save storage space or transmission time.
    </p>    
    <p>
      Data compression is the process of reducing the size of data to save storage space or transmission time. It is an important concept in computer science and is used in many applications, such as image and video compression.
    </p>
    <p>
      In this lesson we continue the exploration of bits and binary numbers that we began in Unit 1. In this case we learn how to use bits, 1s and 0s, to represent images.
    </p>
  </introduction>



<subsection xml:id="unit3a-data-representation">
<title>Data Representation</title>
<p>
  All data in a computer is represented in binary, using 1s and 0s. How it is represented depends on the type of data. For example, text is represented using ASCII, and images are represented using pixels.
</p>

<p>
  All data is stored in bits, which are the smallest unit of data in a computer. A <term>bit</term> can have a value of either 0 or 1. A group of 8 bits is called a <term>byte</term>, which is 8 bits. A byte can represent 256 different values, from 0 to 255. For example, the letter "A" is represented as 01000001 in binary, which is 65 in decimal.
</p>

<p>
  <term>American Standard Code for Information Interchange (ASCII)</term> is character code that includes 128 characters. It allows us to represent text in binary format. Check out <url href="http://sticksandstones.kstrom.com/appen.html">this ASCII to binary table</url> to see how each character is represented in binary. For example, the letter "A" is represented as 01000001 in binary.
</p>

    <activity label="fillin-">
